1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,322
Welcome to lesson two: Linux and networking basics.

2
00:00:04,422 --> 00:00:13,152
Containers are Linux processes, and most Kubernetes problems turn out to be networking problems. So these skills will help you in every lesson that follows.

3
00:00:14,502 --> 00:00:15,984
Why does this matter?

4
00:00:16,084 --> 00:00:23,614
A container is just a Linux process. When a Pod breaks, you'll open a shell inside it and debug with ordinary Linux commands.

5
00:00:23,714 --> 00:00:32,140
And most problems you'll meet are networking problems: the wrong port, a DNS failure, or an app that isn't listening where you think it is.

6
00:00:33,490 --> 00:00:39,148
To practice, open the Killercoda Ubuntu playground again. It's free and needs no installation.

7
00:00:39,248 --> 00:00:41,194
Try each command as we go.

8
00:00:42,544 --> 00:00:45,402
First, moving around files and folders.

9
00:00:45,502 --> 00:00:52,376
pwd shows where you are. ls dash l a lists files, including hidden ones. cd changes directory.

10
00:00:52,476 --> 00:00:56,182
cat prints a file, and less lets you scroll through a long one.

11
00:00:56,282 --> 00:01:04,980
mkdir creates folders, cp copies, mv moves or renames, and rm removes. Be careful with rm, there's no undo.

12
00:01:06,330 --> 00:01:08,628
Next, searching and filtering.

13
00:01:08,728 --> 00:01:15,282
grep finds lines that contain some text. tail dash f follows a log file live, as new lines arrive.

14
00:01:15,382 --> 00:01:27,024
The pipe symbol sends one command's output into the next command. For example, kubectl get pods, piped into grep api, shows only the API Pods. You'll use pipes all the time.

15
00:01:28,374 --> 00:01:34,608
Every program runs as a process. ps lists them, and top shows live CPU and memory use.

16
00:01:34,708 --> 00:01:43,966
kill sends a signal. Plain kill sends SIGTERM, which politely asks the process to stop. kill dash nine sends SIGKILL, which ends it immediately.

17
00:01:44,066 --> 00:01:56,828
This matters in Kubernetes. When a Pod stops, Kubernetes sends SIGTERM, waits thirty seconds by default, and then sends SIGKILL. A well-written app finishes its work and exits when it receives SIGTERM.

18
00:01:58,178 --> 00:02:03,260
Every file has an owner, a group, and permissions for read, write and execute.

19
00:02:03,360 --> 00:02:09,082
chmod changes the permissions, chown changes the owner, and id shows which user you are.

20
00:02:09,182 --> 00:02:19,416
In Kubernetes you can choose which user a container runs as. A permission denied error inside a Pod usually means a file belongs to root, while the app runs as a different user.

21
00:02:20,766 --> 00:02:24,584
Environment variables are named settings that a process can read.

22
00:02:24,684 --> 00:02:28,710
export sets one, echo prints it, and env lists them all.

23
00:02:28,810 --> 00:02:37,572
Kubernetes uses environment variables to pass configuration into containers, from ConfigMaps and Secrets. You'll see that in lesson seven.

24
00:02:38,922 --> 00:02:47,060
Now networking. An IP address identifies a machine, or in Kubernetes, a Pod. A port identifies one app on that machine.

25
00:02:47,160 --> 00:02:56,482
Some ports are worth remembering. Fifty-three is DNS, eighty and four forty-three are web traffic, and sixty-four forty-three is the Kubernetes API server.

26
00:02:56,582 --> 00:03:11,328
Two special addresses matter a lot. 127.0.0.1, also called localhost, means only this machine. 0.0.0.0, when an app listens on it, means accept connections from anywhere.

27
00:03:12,678 --> 00:03:15,200
This leads to one of the most common bugs.

28
00:03:15,300 --> 00:03:26,686
If an app inside a container listens on 127.0.0.1, nothing outside that container can reach it. Not even a perfectly configured Kubernetes Service.

29
00:03:26,786 --> 00:03:34,700
The app must listen on 0.0.0.0 instead. You can check what's listening with the command ss dash t l n p.

30
00:03:34,800 --> 00:03:40,250
AI-generated code often makes this mistake, because many frameworks default to localhost.

31
00:03:41,600 --> 00:03:46,522
Pods and Services use private address ranges, which only work inside your network.

32
00:03:46,622 --> 00:03:56,392
You'll often see addresses written in CIDR notation, like 10.0.0.0 slash twenty-four. The number after the slash says how many bits are fixed.

33
00:03:56,492 --> 00:04:03,558
Slash twenty-four gives two hundred fifty-six addresses. Slash sixteen gives sixty-five thousand five hundred thirty-six.

34
00:04:04,908 --> 00:04:13,734
DNS turns names into IP addresses. nslookup and dig let you test it, and the file resolv dot conf shows which DNS server is used.

35
00:04:13,834 --> 00:04:19,060
Inside Kubernetes, a component called CoreDNS answers names for Services.

36
00:04:19,160 --> 00:04:30,898
Traffic travels over TCP, which is reliable and used by web servers and databases, or over UDP, which is faster but has no delivery guarantee. DNS mostly uses UDP.

37
00:04:32,248 --> 00:04:36,178
When you test a web app with curl, the status code tells you a lot.

38
00:04:36,278 --> 00:04:43,280
Two hundred means OK. Four oh one and four oh three are about permissions. Four oh four means the path wasn't found.

39
00:04:43,380 --> 00:04:52,814
In Kubernetes, five oh two often means the Ingress can't reach the Pods, five oh three means no Pods are ready, and five oh four means the app is too slow.

40
00:04:54,164 --> 00:04:56,526
Here's your network debugging toolkit.

41
00:04:56,626 --> 00:05:02,300
ping checks whether a host is reachable. curl dash v tests HTTP and shows every detail.

42
00:05:02,400 --> 00:05:07,946
nc dash z v checks whether a TCP port is open, which is handy for databases.

43
00:05:08,046 --> 00:05:13,432
And ss dash t l n p shows what's listening on this machine, and on which address.

44
00:05:14,782 --> 00:05:21,048
Let's try it. Create a Docker network, then start an nginx container called api on that network.

45
00:05:21,148 --> 00:05:28,342
Now run a second, temporary container on the same network, and fetch the page at http colon slash slash api.

46
00:05:28,442 --> 00:05:37,684
It works, because Docker's built-in DNS turned the name api into an IP address. Kubernetes Services work in the same way, as you'll see in lesson six.

47
00:05:37,784 --> 00:05:40,418
Finally, clean up the container and the network.

48
00:05:41,768 --> 00:05:43,010
Let's recap.

49
00:05:43,110 --> 00:05:49,936
Containers are Linux processes, so processes, signals, permissions and environment variables all apply.

50
00:05:50,036 --> 00:05:54,078
Kubernetes stops a Pod with SIGTERM first, then SIGKILL.

51
00:05:54,178 --> 00:05:59,772
Apps in containers must listen on 0.0.0.0, not localhost.

52
00:05:59,872 --> 00:06:07,418
And curl, nc, ss and nslookup, plus HTTP status codes, will lead you to the broken layer.

53
00:06:07,518 --> 00:06:12,904
Next, lesson three covers YAML, the language every Kubernetes object is written in.
